# The *16 programs of GR-DOS's full distribution, C:\DOS and the release
# ZIP (issue #36, docs/dist16.md). Every gr-dos repository whose name ends
# in 16 is found on GitHub, so a new gr-dos/foo16 needs no change here;
# this file holds only the exceptions and additions, each with its reason.
# After a change, run `make dist16-resolve` and commit dist16/lock with it.

discover gr-dos 16

# A repository named lib*16 (any case), such as gr-dos/libc16, the C
# runtime GR-DOS's FDISK, ATTRIB, MEM and FC are linked with, or
# gr-dos/libkitchen16, is a library, not a DOS program: discover excludes
# it without an exclude here (issue #61), and an include overrides that.
# Only the library as a program is left out: its source, as a program's
# depends in dist16.source (patch16's libc16), still ships.
# hello16 is the template the other gr-dos *16 repositories are made from;
# HELLO.EXE only prints a greeting.
exclude gr-dos/hello16 the template for gr-dos *16 programs, not a utility

# The tico16 text editor, from another organization. It is on every boot
# floppy as TICO.EXE, and the 360 KB disk's files are frozen (issue #17),
# so it is pinned, and a release of it is taken only by changing tag= here.
include tico-edit/tico16 tag=v0.03 required

# SPROCKET.EXE, the DOS program of plicease/sprocket (issue #37): serial
# file transfer and disk imaging, as client or server. The repository is
# not a *16 one, and its releases also hold the Linux and Windows host
# programs, so asset= names the DOS one. It links libc16, not Open
# Watcom's runtime, so its license notice goes with it: each release has
# it as sprocket-exe-license.txt, the same text as its LICENSE (and
# libc16's), shipped beside it as SPROCKET.LIC.
include plicease/sprocket asset=sprocket.exe license-asset=sprocket-exe-license.txt

# gr-dos/retrofetch16's program is spelled RETROFCH.EXE, since
# retrofetch.exe is not an 8.3 name, so it is not the repository's stem
# (retrofetch) that discovery looks for, and asset= names it.
include gr-dos/retrofetch16 asset=retrofch.exe

# gr-dos/which16 releases both the C reference build, which.exe, and an
# assembly one, which.com: 2,545 bytes to 4,252 in v0.04, both 8086 and
# DOS 2.0+, and compared against the C one in which16's tests. v0.04 has
# its heap-exhaustion fix (which16 PR #9), so the .COM is shipped
# (issue #80). Only which16's: the default stays exe.
include gr-dos/which16 prefer=com

# The licenses a shipped program may have (GitHub's SPDX identifiers):
# permissive ones, whose notice the release ZIP's README.txt carries.
license MIT BSD-2-Clause BSD-3-Clause ISC Zlib 0BSD TCL

# GitHub's license detector does not recognize the Tcl license (TCL), and
# reports NOASSERTION for gr-dos/tcl16, whose LICENSE is it (issue #83).
# Taken as TCL only while v0.01's LICENSE, at its commit, is these exact
# bytes; a release with another LICENSE fails until this is updated after
# reading it. Its LICENSE names Tiny Tcl's own notice, which TCL.EXE also
# carries, so it is shipped with it.
license-override gr-dos/tcl16 TCL LICENSE sha256=b9ee1e757486a359ba286d22c007f054f9a97cc140c05c0e968ff4c67b918689 notice=vendor/LICENSE.tinytcl

# And those that also require the program's complete corresponding source
# to be available (issue #56): GPL-2.0-or-later for gr-dos/patch16, GNU
# patch 2.2, and GPL-1.0-or-later for gr-dos/perl16's Perl 3 when it has a
# release. A program under one ships only with a dist16.source manifest
# naming its source archive, build instructions and the source of each
# library linked into it (docs/dist16.md); its source goes in the release
# ZIP's SOURCE, and C:\DOS\SOURCES.TXT says where to get it. GR-DOS is an
# aggregation of separately licensed programs: this does not change the
# license of the kernel or of any other program.
source-license GPL-2.0-or-later GPL-1.0-or-later
